AB  - Freshwater resources are scarce in arid coastal watersheds due to seawater intrusion exacerbated by groundwater overexploitation. This study implements the quasi-3D finite element model MELEF-FSW to evaluate freshwater-saltwater interactions, explicitly considering tidal influence, sea level rise, climate variability, and anthropogenic pressures. The model was manually calibrated and validated (1995–2017) achieving a satisfactory global performance with groundwater levels (R2 = 0.98, RMSE = 6.49 m) and was verified against previous intrusion studies (R2 = 0.64). Key findings reveal a persistent increase in seawater intrusion in coastal areas despite water resource recovery in mountain blocks, a phenomenon explained by extreme climate events and rising sea levels. Furthermore, the model identifies that urban water supply leakage reduces local intrusion, while intensive groundwater extraction exacerbates it in the southern agricultural region. This model provides a robust, computationally efficient tool for representing transitional fresh-saltwater interactions in integrated surface-groundwater systems, offering critical insights for sustainable water resource management in arid coastal regions.
