AB  - Groundwater level (GWL) in karst areas fluctuates significantly, and non-Darcy seepage complicates prediction. This study leverages the natural adaptability of Liquid Time-Constant Neural Networks (LTC) to ordinary differential equations and combines it with water balance equation to form a physics-feature-enhanced model. It enhances the inference capability of groundwater movement processes by integrating physics-based features that are optimized through backpropagation. Key findings include: (1) In Linyi, LTC_WB outperformed both LTC and LSTM models in 23 of 35 observation wells across hidden and exposed karst aquifers. (2) The model showed strong reliance on the water balance equation's output, which also highlighted other features. (3) Analysis indicated that the exposed aquifer's dependence on precipitation increased, while the hidden aquifer's decreased. Following extreme precipitation, GWL prediction errors were reduced by 0.21m and 0.71m in concealed and exposed aquifers, respectively. This study provides valuable insights into improving model accuracy, interpretability, and regional responses to precipitation.
