AB  - This study presents SOFT CUBE (Synthesis of Flows and Temperatures based on a CFD model and urban building environments), a method for generating high-resolution 3D meteorological fields in urban areas. To reduce the computational cost of CFD, SOFT CUBE reconstructs wind and air temperature fields using a pre-computed scenario database integrated with LDAPS forecasts. The database includes 6640 simulations combining wind speed, direction, land-cover-dependent thermal contrasts, and vertical gradients. Evaluation over 4416 cases shows that SOFT CUBE reduces RMSE by 71 % in wind speed and 17 % in air temperature compared to LDAPS. It also shortens runtime from 141 min to 3 min per case versus the LDAPS–CFD model. Although accuracy declines under strong vertical mixing, results remain statistically consistent with full CFD simulations. These findings demonstrate that SOFT CUBE is a scalable and efficient alternative for urban meteorological modeling, with potential applications in heat-risk assessment and air-quality forecasting.
